<h1>Add your extension</h1>

<p>
    Open VSIX Gallery is a simple HTTP API. You can publish a <code>.vsix</code> file
    by uploading it from a script, a manual <code>curl</code> command, or a CI system
    like GitHub Actions or AppVeyor. Pick whichever fits your project.
</p>

<ol>
    <li><a href="#how-it-works">How publishing works</a></li>
    <li><a href="#manual">Upload manually with curl</a></li>
    <li><a href="#powershell">Use PowerShell</a></li>
    <li><a href="#github-actions">Automate with GitHub Actions</a> (recommended)</li>
    <li><a href="#appveyor">Use AppVeyor</a></li>
    <li><a href="#manage">Managing your extension</a></li>
    <li><a href="#unlisted">Unlisted extensions</a></li>
    <li><a href="#badges">Show a version badge</a></li>
    <li><a href="/guidelines">Validation guidelines</a></li>
</ol>

<section id="how-it-works">
    <h2>How publishing works</h2>

    <p>
        Publishing is a single <code>POST</code> to
        <code>https://www.vsixgallery.com/api/upload</code> with the <code>.vsix</code>
        file as a multipart form upload. The server reads the manifest, stores the package,
        and returns a JSON response with the extension URL and a manage URL.
    </p>

    <p>Optional headers and query-string parameters:</p>

    <ul>
        <li>
            <code>X-Manage-Token</code> &mdash; a value of your choice that becomes
            the &ldquo;password&rdquo; for the extension&rsquo;s manage page. If you
            omit it on the first upload, the server generates one and embeds it in
            the manage URL it returns. See <a href="#manage">Managing your extension</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
            <code>?repo=&lt;url&gt;</code> &mdash; the source repository URL.
        </li>
        <li>
            <code>?issuetracker=&lt;url&gt;</code> &mdash; the issue tracker URL.
        </li>
        <li>
            <code>?readmeUrl=&lt;url-or-path&gt;</code> &mdash; an HTTPS URL on
            <code>raw.githubusercontent.com</code> or <code>gist.githubusercontent.com</code>,
            or a path such as <code>main/README.md</code> relative to the GitHub repository
            supplied by <code>repo</code>.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        Once that one HTTP call works, automating it with PowerShell, GitHub Actions,
        or AppVeyor is just glue around the same call.
    </p>
</section>

<section id="manual">
    <h2>Upload manually with curl</h2>

    <p>
        Useful for one-off uploads or for trying the API before wiring it into a build.
    </p>

<pre>curl --fail-with-body -X POST "https://www.vsixgallery.com/api/upload?repo=https://github.com/me/MyExtension" \
     -H "X-Manage-Token: my-secret-token" \
     -F "file=@@MyExtension.vsix"</pre>

    <p>
        The response is JSON containing the extension page URL, manage URL, and any
        non-blocking quality warnings found while inspecting the package.
        On the very first upload of a new extension ID, if you didn&rsquo;t supply
        <code>X-Manage-Token</code>, the manage URL will contain a one-time token
        as a query string &mdash; save the URL.
    </p>

    <details class="example">
        <summary>Example upload response with validation warnings</summary>
<pre>{
  "id": "Example.Extension",
  "name": "Example Extension",
  "version": "1.0",
  "manageUrl": "https://www.vsixgallery.com/extension/Example.Extension/manage",
  "validation": [
    {
      "severity": "warning",
      "code": "icon.invalid-dimensions",
      "message": "The source icon is 48x48px. For best results, use 128x128 pixels or larger. Larger images are supported but are never displayed above 128x128 pixels."
    }
  ]
}</pre>
    </details>

    <p>
        Warnings do not prevent publication. Invalid or unsafe packages are rejected with
        HTTP <code>400</code> and expose the same <code>validation</code> shape through
        the response&rsquo;s Problem Details object. See the
        <a href="/guidelines">validation guidelines</a> for every warning rule and its justification.
    </p>
</section>

<section id="powershell">
    <h2>Upload with PowerShell</h2>

    <p>
        PowerShell 7 can upload the VSIX directly, without downloading or executing a remote script.
        Store the manage token in a secret environment variable rather than in source control.
    </p>

<pre>$headers = @@{ "X-Manage-Token" = $env:VSIX_GALLERY_TOKEN }
$form = @@{ file = Get-Item ".\src\bin\Release\MyExtension.vsix" }
Invoke-RestMethod `
    -Uri "https://www.vsixgallery.com/api/upload?repo=https://github.com/me/MyExtension" `
    -Method Post `
    -Headers $headers `
    -Form $form</pre>
</section>

<section id="github-actions">
    <h2>Automate with GitHub Actions</h2>

    <p>
        GitHub Actions is the recommended way to build and publish your Visual Studio extension.
        It provides free CI/CD directly in your GitHub repository, and the
        <a href="https://github.com/madskristensen/publish-vsixgallery">publish-vsixgallery</a>
        action wraps the <code>POST /api/upload</code> call described above.
    </p>

    <h3>Add publishing to an existing workflow</h3>
    <p>
        If your workflow already builds a VSIX, add this step after the build. Store the manage
        token as a repository secret named <code>VSIX_GALLERY_TOKEN</code>.
    </p>

<pre>- name: Upload to Open VSIX Gallery
  uses: madskristensen/publish-vsixgallery@@v1
  with:
    vsix-file: src\bin\Release\MyExtension.vsix
    manage-token: ${{ secrets.VSIX_GALLERY_TOKEN }}</pre>

    <p>
        If you need a complete starting point, create
        <code>.github/workflows/build.yaml</code> and adapt the following workflow to your
        solution, project paths, branch name, and publishing secrets:
    </p>

    <details class="example">
        <summary>Full workflow example (build, test, publish, release)</summary>
<pre># yaml-language-server: $schema=https://www.schemastore.org/github-workflow.json
name: "Build"
permissions:
  actions: write
  contents: write

on:
  push:
    branches: [master]
  pull_request:
    branches: [master]
  workflow_dispatch:

jobs:
  build:
    outputs:
      version: ${{ steps.vsix_version.outputs.version-number }}
    name: Build
    runs-on: windows-latest
    env:
      Configuration: Release
      DeployExtension: False
      Solution: MyExtension.sln
      TestProject: tests\MyExtension.Tests\MyExtension.Tests.csproj
      VsixManifestPath: src\source.extension.vsixmanifest
      VsixManifestSourcePath: src\source.extension.cs

    steps:
    - uses: actions/checkout@v6

    - name: Setup MSBuild
      uses: microsoft/setup-msbuild@v3

    - name: Cache NuGet packages
      uses: actions/cache@v5
      with:
        path: ~/.nuget/packages
        key: ${{ runner.os }}-nuget-${{ hashFiles('**/*.slnx', '**/*.sln', '**/*.csproj', '**/packages.config', '**/packages.lock.json', '**/nuget.config') }}
        restore-keys: |
          ${{ runner.os }}-nuget-

    - name: Increment VSIX version
      id: vsix_version
      uses: madskristensen/vsix-version-stamp@v1.1
      with:
        manifest-file: ${{ env.VsixManifestPath }}
        vsix-token-source-file: ${{ env.VsixManifestSourcePath }}

    - name: Restore NuGet packages
      run: msbuild ${{ env.Solution }} /v:m -restore /t:Restore

    - name: Build
      run: msbuild ${{ env.Solution }} /v:m /m /p:Configuration=${{ env.Configuration }} /p:DeployExtension=${{ env.DeployExtension }}

    - name: Run tests
      run: dotnet test ${{ env.TestProject }} --configuration ${{ env.Configuration }} --no-build --no-restore --verbosity normal --logger:"trx;LogFileName=test-results.trx"

    - name: Report test results
      uses: dorny/test-reporter@v3
      if: ${{ always() &amp;&amp; (github.event_name != 'pull_request' || github.event.pull_request.head.repo.full_name == github.repository) }}
      with:
        name: Test Results
        path: '**/test-results.trx'
        reporter: dotnet-trx

    - name: Upload artifact
      uses: actions/upload-artifact@v7
      with:
        name: ${{ github.event.repository.name }}.vsix
        path: src\bin\${{ env.Configuration }}\net48\MyExtension.vsix

  publish:
    if: ${{ github.event_name == 'push' || github.event_name == 'workflow_dispatch' }}
    needs: build
    runs-on: windows-latest

    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v6
        with:
          fetch-depth: 1

      - name: Download Package artifact
        uses: actions/download-artifact@v8
        with:
          name: ${{ github.event.repository.name }}.vsix

      - name: Upload to Open VSIX
        uses: madskristensen/publish-vsixgallery@v1
        with:
          vsix-file: ${{ github.event.repository.name }}.vsix
          manage-token: ${{ secrets.VSIX_GALLERY_TOKEN }}

      - name: Publish extension to Marketplace
        if: ${{ github.event_name == 'workflow_dispatch' || contains(github.event.head_commit.message, '[release]') }}
        uses: madskristensen/publish-marketplace@v1.16
        with:
          extension-file: '${{ github.event.repository.name }}.vsix'
          publish-manifest-file: 'vs-publish.json'
          personal-access-code: ${{ secrets.VS_PUBLISHER_ACCESS_TOKEN }}

      - name: Tag and release
        if: ${{ github.event_name == 'workflow_dispatch' || contains(github.event.head_commit.message, '[release]') }}
        id: tag_release
        uses: softprops/action-gh-release@v3
        with:
          body: release ${{ needs.build.outputs.version }}
          generate_release_notes: true
          tag_name: ${{ needs.build.outputs.version }}
          files: |
            **/*.vsix
</pre>
    </details>

    <h3>Key GitHub Actions</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="https://github.com/microsoft/setup-msbuild">microsoft/setup-msbuild</a> &mdash; adds MSBuild to the runner&rsquo;s PATH</li>
        <li><a href="https://github.com/madskristensen/vsix-version-stamp">madskristensen/vsix-version-stamp</a> &mdash; auto-increments the VSIX version number and stamps it into the manifest</li>
        <li><a href="https://github.com/dorny/test-reporter">dorny/test-reporter</a> &mdash; renders test results in the workflow run summary</li>
        <li><a href="https://github.com/madskristensen/publish-vsixgallery">madskristensen/publish-vsixgallery</a> &mdash; publishes to Open VSIX Gallery (writes a run summary with the extension link, badge, and a self-service manage link for deleting the extension)</li>
        <li><a href="https://github.com/madskristensen/publish-marketplace">madskristensen/publish-marketplace</a> &mdash; publishes to the Visual Studio Marketplace</li>
        <li><a href="https://github.com/softprops/action-gh-release">softprops/action-gh-release</a> &mdash; creates a GitHub release and attaches the .vsix as an asset</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Real-world examples using GitHub Actions:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="https://github.com/madskristensen/InstaSearch/blob/master/.github/workflows/build.yaml">InstaSearch</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://github.com/madskristensen/AddAnyFile/blob/master/.github/workflows/build.yaml">Add Any File</a></li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section id="appveyor">
    <h2>Use AppVeyor</h2>

    <p>AppVeyor is a build server hosted in the cloud and it&rsquo;s free.</p>

    <p>
        After you&rsquo;ve created an account, you can start doing automated builds. A really nice thing
        is that AppVeyor can automatically kick off a new build when you commit code to either
        GitHub, VSO or other code repositories.
    </p>

    <p>
        To automatically upload your extension to vsixgallery.com when the build has succeeded,
        all you have to do is to add an <strong>appveyor.yml</strong> file to the root of
        your repository. The content of the file should look like this:
    </p>

    <details class="example">
        <summary>appveyor.yml example</summary>
<pre>version: 1.0.{build}

build_script:
  - msbuild /p:configuration=Release /p:DeployExtension=false /p:ZipPackageCompressionLevel=normal /v:m

after_test:
  - cmd: curl.exe --fail-with-body -X POST "https://www.vsixgallery.com/api/upload" -H "X-Manage-Token: %VSIX_GALLERY_TOKEN%" -F "file=@@src\bin\Release\MyExtension.vsix"
</pre>
    </details>

    <p>You might want to check out these real-world uses:</p>

    <ul>
        <li><a href="https://github.com/madskristensen/WebEssentials2019/blob/master/appveyor.yml">Web Essentials 2019</a></li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section id="manage">
    <h2>Managing your extension</h2>

    <p>
        Every extension has a manage page at
        <code>https://www.vsixgallery.com/extension/&lt;id&gt;/manage</code>
        where you (the publisher) can delete it. Access is gated by a per-extension
        <strong>manage token</strong> that acts as a password. Treat it like any other
        publishing credential: store it in a secret manager and do not commit it to source control.
    </p>

    <p>How the token gets set depends on what you do at upload time. This applies
       to <em>any</em> upload method &mdash; manual <code>curl</code>, PowerShell,
       GitHub Actions, AppVeyor, etc.</p>

    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>First upload, no token supplied</strong> &mdash; the gallery
            auto-generates a token and embeds it in the manage URL it returns
            (printed by the GitHub Action&rsquo;s run summary, or visible in the
            JSON response for manual uploads). That URL is shown only once,
            so save it somewhere safe.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>First upload, token supplied</strong> &mdash; the gallery
            stores the value you supply (typically a CI secret). The manage URL
            won&rsquo;t contain it.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Republish, no token supplied</strong> &mdash; the existing
            token stays in place. The manage URL won&rsquo;t contain it.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Republish, token supplied</strong> &mdash; whatever value you
            pass becomes the new manage token, replacing whatever was on file.
            Lost your old token? Just republish with a fresh one and the new
            value takes over.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        With the GitHub Action, the token is supplied via the
        <code>manage-token</code> input. With <code>curl</code> it&rsquo;s the
        <code>X-Manage-Token</code> request header. Same value either way.
    </p>

    </section>

<section id="unlisted">
    <h2>Unlisted extensions</h2>

    <p>
        An unlisted extension is hidden from discovery surfaces. You need its direct link to find it.
        It remains available from:
    </p>

    <ul>
        <li>Direct link (example: vsixgallery.com/extension/[ID])</li>
        <li>Extension gallery feed</li>
    </ul>

    <p>That means it won&rsquo;t show up in the usual places on this website such as:</p>

    <ul>
        <li>Front page</li>
        <li>Search results</li>
        <li>Author page</li>
        <li>Author gallery feed</li>
        <li>Main gallery feed</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        To unlist an extension, simply add the tag &ldquo;unlisted&rdquo; in the .vsixmanifest file.
        The tags specified in the .vsixmanifest are not shown on either the Open VSIX Gallery nor inside Visual Studio,
        so it won&rsquo;t be visible to anyone.
    </p>

    <p>Here&rsquo;s what it could look like:</p>

    <pre>&lt;Tags&gt;foo, bar, unlisted&lt;/Tags&gt;</pre>
</section>

<section id="badges">
    <h2>Show a version badge</h2>

    <p>
        Every extension exposes a live badge that always reflects the latest
        published version. Drop it in your <code>README</code> to show visitors
        the current version at a glance and link straight to the gallery page.
    </p>

    <p>Two formats are available &mdash; just change the file extension:</p>

    <ul>
        <li>
            <code>https://www.vsixgallery.com/badge/&lt;id&gt;.svg</code> &mdash;
            scalable vector, stays crisp at any size (recommended).
        </li>
        <li>
            <code>https://www.vsixgallery.com/badge/&lt;id&gt;.png</code> &mdash;
            rasterized image for places that don&rsquo;t render SVG. Notably, the
            Visual Studio Marketplace only allows SVG badges from a short list of
            approved sources, so use the PNG variant there (and in other places
            that block third-party SVGs, such as some chat clients or release notes).
        </li>
    </ul>

    <p>To embed the badge as a clickable link in Markdown:</p>

    <pre>[![Install from VSIX Gallery](https://www.vsixgallery.com/badge/&lt;id&gt;.svg)](https://www.vsixgallery.com/extension/&lt;id&gt;/)</pre>

    <p>Or with the PNG variant:</p>

    <pre>[![Install from VSIX Gallery](https://www.vsixgallery.com/badge/&lt;id&gt;.png)](https://www.vsixgallery.com/extension/&lt;id&gt;/)</pre>

    <p>
        Replace <code>&lt;id&gt;</code> with your extension&rsquo;s ID. The easiest
        way to grab the ready-made Markdown is the <strong>⚙️ Actions</strong> menu
        on any extension page &mdash; pick <strong>Copy badge (SVG)</strong> or
        <strong>Copy badge (PNG)</strong> and it&rsquo;s on your clipboard.
    </p>
</section>
